# Chapter 9 — The Beat

Last chapter you gave the console a voice — a *ding*, a *boing*, a hum you could
start and stop. But a pile of dings is not music. What turns single sounds into a
song is *timing*: playing them on a steady pulse, so the ear feels a rhythm
instead of hearing scattered noise. That pulse is the **beat**, and it is the
subject of this whole chapter.

Here is the secret that ties it all together: the beat is not a new idea. It is
the tick from Chapter 5, counted in music instead of frames. The console already
has a heartbeat — sixty frames a second. The beat is just a *slower* heartbeat,
one you set the speed of, and one the whole song marches to.

## The beat has a speed

Every song has a tempo — how fast the beat goes. The console keeps one, measured
in **BPM** (*beats per minute*), and `audio/tempo` reads it:

```motoi
(display (audio/tempo))
(newline)
```

```
120
```

`120` means a hundred and twenty beats every minute — a brisk, danceable pace,
and the console's default. This is the number the whole music system marches to:
every drum hit, every note, every *at-the-right-moment* is measured against it.
Read it any time you want to know how fast the song is moving.

## From beats to seconds

A minute has sixty seconds, so a hundred and twenty beats a minute is **two beats
every second**. You do not have to take that on faith — the console can do the
arithmetic:

```motoi
(display (/ (audio/tempo) 60))
(newline)
```

```
2
```

Divide the tempo by the sixty seconds in a minute, and you get beats per second:
`2`. That is the first bridge — from the musician's unit (beats per minute) to
the clock's unit (seconds). At `120` BPM, two beats tick by in every second that
passes.

## From beats to frames — the bridge to the tick

Now the bridge that matters most. Chapter 5's heartbeat runs at sixty frames a
second. If two beats also happen each second, then one beat lasts *thirty
frames*. The beat and the tick are the same clock, just counted differently — and
you can prove it:

```motoi
(display (round (* (/ 60 (audio/tempo)) 60)))
(newline)
```

```
30
```

Read that inside-out: `(/ 60 (audio/tempo))` is how many *seconds* one beat lasts
— a half-second at `120` BPM. Multiply by the sixty frames in a second, and one
beat is `30` frames long. **That is the whole idea of this chapter.** *On the
beat* means *every thirtieth frame*. The musical pulse is the frame-tick from
Chapter 5, gathered up into bigger, slower steps you can dance to.

## Change the speed, and the frames follow

Because frames-per-beat is *computed* from the tempo, changing the tempo changes
it. Reading and setting use slightly different names here — `audio/tempo` to
read, `audio-tempo` to set — so slow the song to `90` BPM and ask again:

```motoi
(audio-tempo 90)
(display (round (* (/ 60 (audio/tempo)) 60)))
(newline)
```

```
40
```

A slower song stretches each beat wider: at `90` BPM one beat now spans `40`
frames instead of `30`. Speed the tempo up and the number shrinks; slow it down
and it grows. The console is not remembering a fact — it is recomputing the
bridge every time from the tempo you set. Change the pulse, and every beat in the
song re-measures itself against the tick.

## Snap a moment to the beat

Music feels right when things land *on* the beat, not between two. When you have
a moment that falls at a fuzzy in-between spot — beat `2.7`, say — you often want
to nudge it to the nearest whole beat. `land-on-downbeat` does exactly that:

```motoi
(display (land-on-downbeat 2.7))
(newline)
```

```
3
```

`2.7` was closer to beat `3` than to beat `2`, so it snapped up to `3`. This is
how a rhythm game forgives a slightly-early or slightly-late tap: it rounds the
player's moment to the beat the music meant. A small verb, but it is the
difference between a hit that feels sloppy and one that feels *tight*.

## Schedule a sound on the beat

Now the move the whole chapter has been walking toward: play a sound *on* a beat.
`beat/on` takes a beat number and a job to run when that beat arrives — the same
shape as `world/after` from Chapter 5, but counted in beats instead of frames.
Keep its handle the way you kept a sound's handle in Chapter 8:

```motoi
(define kick (beat/on 4 (lambda () (audio/play 'kick))))
(display 'beat-scheduled)
(newline)
```

```
beat-scheduled
```

You told the console: *when beat `4` comes around, play the kick drum.* Like the
scheduled job in Chapter 5 and the button reads in Chapter 6, this needs a
running console to fire — a live music loop counting beats is what triggers it,
and there is none at the bare prompt. So we schedule the job and print
`beat-scheduled` to show the line ran. In a playing console, beat `4` arrives and
the kick sounds, dead on the pulse.

## What you just proved

You found the beat. You read the tempo (`120` BPM), turned it into beats per
second (`2`), and built the bridge to Chapter 5's tick — one beat is `30` frames
at the default speed, `40` when you slow the song to `90` BPM. You snapped a
loose moment to the nearest whole beat, and you scheduled a drum to play *on* a
beat. Every program ran and reported a real number you could check against the
arithmetic yourself.

A single beat is a metronome. What makes it a *song* is layering — a kick here, a
snare there, a note held across several beats — and doing it frame after frame
while the rest of the world moves. You now have the pulse; Chapter 14 is where
you build a whole tune on top of it. But first, the eye: the next chapters give
your moving world *animation*, so a walking hero actually looks like it is
walking.

> **Tip**
>
> When something musical feels off, do the frames-per-beat arithmetic first:
> `(round (* (/ 60 (audio/tempo)) 60))`. If your sound is meant to land every
> beat but you scheduled it every `30` frames while the tempo is `90`, the two
> no longer match — a beat is `40` frames now. Let the tempo drive the number;
> never hard-code the frame count.

> **Warning**
>
> `beat/on` schedules a job; it does not run it here. At the prompt no music loop
> is counting beats, so the job waits and never fires — exactly like the
> `world/after` job in Chapter 5 and the button reads in Chapter 6. This is not a
> broken schedule; it is an un-pumped one. To hear a beat actually land, run
> inside a live console. Appendix E lays out the synth, the tempo clock, and the
> beat scheduler in full.

